// Probes for category=lang. 20 atoms × 15 probes = 300 probes.
// Decline notes (issues raised by subagent review and either fixed or accepted):
//
// FIXED:
//  - Replaced verbatim Adverts cloze in lang.multiblank_cloze.L2.001 (PSA-cloze instead).
//  - Replaced canonical "tomatoes/Kannada" example in lang.target_language_compliance.L3.001.
//  - Broadened idiom-translation regexes (translation_pair.L2.001/.002/.003/.005) to
//    accept multiple natural target-language renderings.
//  - Added DOTALL flag to non_latin_script_robustness L1 (sentence count) and L3 (no-comma)
//    regexes so multi-line responses are not falsely rejected.
//  - Added DOTALL/relaxed sentence-end regexes in creative_generation L1.004 (tweet),
//    L2.003 (3 sentences), L2.004 (5 sentences) to allow ! and ? as sentence terminators.
//  - Replaced junk distractor "PEARS-x" in lexical_edit_recognition.L2.003.
//  - Rebalanced MCQ answer-letter distribution for lang.discourse_coherence (was 100% A)
//    and lang.negation_polarity.L3 (was 100% B) — see _rebalance.py.
//  - Updated expository_explanation.L3.003 scorer to accept "DOM", "dom", or
//    "Document Object Model" alongside "reconciliation".
//
// ACCEPTED LIMITATIONS:
//  - For lang.target_language_compliance and lang.translation_pair, language compliance is checked via Unicode script regex or distinctive lexical markers (e.g. "Москва", "Bahnhof"); the model could in principle add extra English commentary alongside, but the prompts ask for short answers so this is rare.
//  - For lang.creative_generation, exact-match scoring is impossible by definition; we use format_check (line/sentence/word counts and structural markers) as the most precise deterministic surrogate.
//  - For lang.expository_explanation, scoring uses substring_all on canonical technical terms that any correct explanation must mention; this is a lower bound on correctness, not a quality measure (acceptable per atom definition).
//  - For lang.referential_grounding L3, prompts use a small candidate set with mutually-exclusive responsibilities so a paraphrase maps deterministically to a unique file. We do NOT depend on multimodal.image_understanding (which is listed as a dep but is not exercised here).
//  - lang.semantic_grouping uses canonical alphabetical normalization in both the prompt and ground truth so an "unordered set of sets" is collapsed to a single deterministic string.
//  - lang.negation_polarity L2/L3 and lang.semantic_grouping L2/L3 unavoidably mix world
//    knowledge with the negation/grouping skill (need to know which animals are mammals,
//    which words are anagrams). The atom cards declare these dependencies implicitly
//    via their evidence excerpts.
//  - lang.semantic_precision L2/L3 reuse a "lying-character" frame across all 5 probes per
//    level. The atom is specifically about resolving lexical-distinct synonymy under a
//    contrast structure (per its evidence quote: "Amy speaks mistruths and Sam lies"),
//    so domain bleed is intrinsic to the atom rather than a probe-design accident.
//  - lang.creative_generation L1/L3 are poem-heavy because the atom levels distinguish
//    short structural forms (haiku, quatrain) at L1 from voice-stylized verse forms
//    (limerick, sonnet, villanelle) at L3 — both are tightly verse-shaped per the atom card.
//  - Script-range regexes in target_language_compliance L2/L3 and translation_pair L3 only
//    require ≥1 character of the target script. This admits English+1-non-Latin-char
//    responses; the prompt explicitly demands monolingual output, so non-compliant
//    behavior is plausible but not specifically enforced beyond the script-presence check.
